++/// Where a rotated texture's image last stood holding the truth at an encoder's end, carried
++/// across command buffers (pdx203): the shadow's [`touched`](super::shadow::ShadowCopies::touched)
++/// after that end, and the rectangles the encoder left held and stale. A rotated texture is
++/// framebuffer-only, so neither the CPU nor a blit writes its plane through Metal; the display's
++/// framebuffers are written by their own passes alone. So a later command buffer's first use takes
++/// the image as it stood instead of copying the plane in again (QuartzCore's landscape frames: a
++/// full copy in and turn every frame, ~30 ms of GPU per frame on an Adreno 650).
++#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
++pub(super) struct LastStanding {
++    pub(super) touched: u64,
++    pub(super) held: Option<[u32; 4]>,
++    pub(super) stale: Option<[u32; 4]>,
++}
++
